This orientation can also be used to study the weak magnetic field of Earth.
The textbook describes using a coil with more turns or a larger current to make the orienting effect easier to observe;

it does not say that every field measurement requires a very large current.
The coil in Figure 245 can turn about both vertical and horizontal axes, so it can show the field direction tilted relative to the ground.

The field at the region discussed in the textbook tilts downward toward the ground;
different regions do not all have the same direction or amount of tilt.

Near a long straight wire carrying current, a magnetic needle settles along a tangent to a circle centered on the wire, as in Figure 248.
The circle lies in a plane perpendicular to the wire;

the field direction at each point is tangent to the circle, not toward the wire.
Let a right-handed screw advance in the current direction: the turning direction of its handle gives the direction of the surrounding magnetic field.

This rule uses conventional current direction;
the electron-motion direction cannot be substituted without reversing the relevant direction.

Magnetic needles or small current-carrying loops can probe other fields too, such as the field of the current-carrying solenoid in Figure 249.
A solenoid is formed by winding a wire into a series of loops;
placing a needle at different locations lets us compare the local field directions.

The similar behavior of a magnetic needle and a small current loop reflects that both can act as magnetic dipoles and orient in an external field.
The textbook uses the picture of many tiny closed currents to explain permanent magnets;

this is a model that helps us reason, while modern physics also considers microscopic magnetic moments and their alignment within a material.
The model does not mean that a needle literally contains tiny metal-wire coils;
the following chapter discusses the related ideas.
